**Why every guest was rebuilt, and what the previous builds showed (the neuters).**
- **Eight guests were built from 944cfd2** (wasmpreempt/phase4, 2026-10-28). Their c1alloc hung at the 60s bound at a
  low rate: 9 in 10,000 iterations on orion.local and 17 in 10,000 on janus.local (#406's stress), and 7 by about
  iteration 1600 on maya.local. In every dump the stall was an idle M asleep in poll_oneoff on a far timer, with the
  others in futex waits nothing notified (#1181).
- **c4lock was built from f608b56** (#1157's fix, 2026-10-29). The same source built with the fork at 944cfd2 throws
  "on a locked thread with no template thread" and exits 2. That is #1157's neuter, run 3 of 3 on 2026-10-29.
- **With all nine rebuilt from 01f3108158**, maya.local's stress (pueue build task 7) gave 0 of 4000 for c1alloc and
  for c4lock. The 10,000-iteration counts on janus and orion are recorded on #1181.
- **c1stw still hangs with this build**, 3 of 3 at a 90s bound. Its hang is #1064's (a spinning goroutine the fork's
  compiler-inserted loop checks don't preempt), not #1181's, so it keeps its pin and its report-only arm (#1172).

Each <case>.expect is stock Go's result for the same source: class, exit code, first stderr line, and stdout. It was
generated by building <case>.go.txt NATIVELY with stock Go (a built binary, not `go run`, which reports its own exit 1
for a program that exits 2) and running it under a 30 s wall clock. TestGoRuntimeTortureExpectationsAreStockGos
re-derives every .expect from stock Go on each run, so an expectation cannot drift from the toolchain it claims.
ctlhung.expect is the one written by hand: stock Go was killed by the same wall clock, and "hung" is what that kill is.
None of the .expect files changed with the rebuild: stock Go's results don't depend on the fork.

Black-box by the chair's ruling (2026-10-28, fifth, item 1): nothing here reads the fork's runtime. The observables
are what each guest prints and how it ends. The fork's fix was made in the fork's own tree, on the chair's ruling of
